            <ArticleTitle>Sick leave reductions from a comprehensive manual therapy programme for low back pain: the Gotland Low Back Pain Study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>529-41</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/0269215507087294</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate if a comprehensive manual therapy programme reduces sick leave due low back pain and facilitates return to work more than the conventional optimized activating care.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A randomized controlled trial over a 10-week period with a two-year follow-up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Primary health care and Visby Hospital, Municipality of Gotland, Sweden.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred and sixty patients (70 women, 90 men, ages 20-55 years) with acute or subacute low back pain with or without pain radiation into the legs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Standardized optimized activating care (n = 71) versus a comprehensive pragmatic manual therapy programme including specific corticosteroid injections (n = 89).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sick leave measured as net sick leave volume, point prevalence and return to work.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">After 10 weeks, significantly more manual therapy patients than reference patients had returned to work (hazards ratio 1.62, 95% confidence interval (CI) 1.006-2.60, P&lt;0.05), and among those on sick leave at baseline, significantly fewer were still on sick leave (8/58 versus 13/40, ratio 0.35, 95% CI 0.13-0.97, P&lt;0.05). For all other measures there were inconclusive differences in favour of the manual therapy group. No significant differences remained after two years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The manual therapy programme used in this study decreased sick leave and increased return to work more than the standardized optimized activating care only up to 10 weeks but not up to two years.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
